{
  "description": "Golden table for keyboard occlusion on interaction tap points (#2589), consumed by packages/contracts/src/tap-keyboard-occlusion.ts. A visible software keyboard is its own system surface: it is never a covering sibling of app content, so the same-window occlusion classifier cannot see it, and it leaves the app window rect intact, so the viewport rule passes a covered tab bar. The band runs from the topmost keyboard node down to the bottom of the viewport, across the keyboard's own columns; the tap point is the target's rect center, matching isTapPointInsideViewport. `nodes` are captured snapshot nodes as they appear in the projection the interaction resolved against — note that regular and interactive projections prune the keyboard's own container, leaving Key nodes beside app chrome. `target` names either a resolved node index or a bare coordinate point; both paths read one decision and choose their own consequence.",
